{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012nymFBnzovqy1kd2GVYaaS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch MLE main_ptrs Upload\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` overhead in MLE sumcheck rounds by consolidating all per-round main_ptrs into a single flat vector and uploading it with one `to_device()` call. This removes ~3000-4000 `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` + `cudaFreeAsync` triplets per benchmark, saving an estimated 20-40ms from the 170ms MLE Rounds span at APC 300.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry point\n10\t\n11\t`LogupZerocheckGpu::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1111`\n12\t\n13\tThis function is called once per MLE round per segment. At APC 300 with 2 segments and ~8 rounds per segment, it executes ~16 times per benchmark.\n14\t\n15\t### Per-trace main_ptrs upload (the bottleneck)\n16\t\n17\t**Case A (late_eval, round > n_lift)** — lines 1188-1195:\n18\t```rust\n19\tlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..]\n20\t    .iter()\n21\t    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: m.buffer().as_ptr(), air_width: ... })\n22\t    .collect_vec();\n23\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // ← per-trace cudaMallocAsync + H2D\n24\t```\n25\t\n26\t**Case B (early_eval, round <= n_lift)** — lines 1335-1347:\n27\t```rust\n28\tlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..]\n29\t    .iter()\n30\t    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: base_ptr.wrapping_add(...), air_width: ... })\n31\t    .collect_vec();\n32\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // ← per-trace cudaMallocAsync + H2D\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\tEach call creates a `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` via:\n36\t1. `d_malloc()` acquires `MEMORY_MANAGER` mutex + `cudaMallocAsync` (~5-10μs)\n37\t2. `copy_to()` → `cudaMemcpyAsync` H2D (~2-5μs)\n38\t\n39\tWhen each `DeviceBuffer` is dropped (at `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` exit), `d_free()` acquires the same mutex + calls `cudaFreeAsync`. So the full per-trace cost is: 2 mutex acquisitions + `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` + `cudaFreeAsync` + HashMap insert/remove, totaling ~15-20μs per trace.\n40\t\n41\t**Count**: Case A fires once per trace across all rounds (~310 per segment × 2 segments = ~620 calls). Case B fires for each trace where `round <= n_lift`, decreasing as rounds progress. Total across all rounds and segments: ~3000-4000 calls. At ~15-20μs per call including deallocation, this is ~45-80ms of overhead, of which ~30-40ms is on the critical path (allocation + copy; deallocation is deferred to function exit).\n42\t\n43\t### How main_ptrs_dev is consumed\n44\t\n45\t**TraceCtx struct** — `batch_mle.rs:89-105`:\n46\t```rust\n47\tpub(crate) struct TraceCtx {\n48\t    // ...\n49\t    pub main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>,  // owns the allocation\n50\t    // ...\n51\t}\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\tAll consumers only use the raw pointer:\n55\t1. **Batch builders** (`batch_mle.rs:188`, `batch_mle.rs:335`, `batch_mle_monomial.rs:184,407,672`): use `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()`\n56\t2. **Single-trace fallback** (`batch_mle.rs:473`): calls `evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu(&t.main_ptrs_dev)`, which internally uses `.as_ptr()` at `mle_round.rs:56`\n57\t3. **Single-trace fallback** (`batch_mle.rs:642`): calls `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu(&t.main_ptrs_dev)`, which internally uses `.as_ptr()` at `mle_round.rs:111`\n58\t\n59\tNeither fallback function uses `.len()` or any other `DeviceBuffer` method beyond `.as_ptr()`.\n60\t\n61\t## Changes\n62\t\n63\t### Change 1: Modify TraceCtx to use raw pointer\n64\t\n65\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:89-105`\n66\t\n67\tReplace:\n68\t```rust\n69\tpub main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>,\n70\t```\n71\tWith:\n72\t```rust\n73\tpub main_ptrs_ptr: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>,\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\t**Why**: TraceCtx no longer owns individual allocations. It holds a pointer into a shared device buffer that is kept alive by the caller.\n77\t\n78\t### Change 2: Update batch builder usage of main_ptrs\n79\t\n80\t**Files**:\n81\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:188` — `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new()`\n82\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:335` — `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new()`\n83\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:184` — `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::new()`\n84\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:407` — `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch::new()`\n85\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:672` — `LogupMonomialBatch::new()`\n86\t\n87\tChange `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr` at each site.\n88\t\n89\tAlso update the doc comment at `batch_mle_monomial.rs:109-110` which references `main_ptrs_dev` by name.\n90\t\n91\t**Why**: Trivial rename. The raw pointer is the same value that `.as_ptr()` returned.\n92\t\n93\t### Change 3: Update fallback evaluation function signatures\n94\t\n95\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mle_round.rs`\n96\t\n97\t`evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu()` (line 24): Change parameter from `d_main_ptrs: &DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to `d_main_ptrs: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>`. Update the call site at line 56 from `d_main_ptrs.as_ptr()` to `d_main_ptrs`.\n98\t\n99\t`evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu()` (line 78): Same change. Update call site at line 111.\n100\t\n101\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n102\t\n103\tUpdate call sites:\n104\t- Line 473: `&t.main_ptrs_dev` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr`\n105\t- Line 642: `&t.main_ptrs_dev` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr`\n106\t\n107\t**Why**: These functions only need the raw pointer. Removing the `DeviceBuffer` reference requirement allows them to work with shared-buffer pointers. This trades a nominal compile-time type-safety guarantee for the performance gain, but both functions are `pub(crate)` internal helpers already operating in an unsafe context (raw device pointers throughout).\n108\t\n109\t### Change 4: Batch main_ptrs upload in sumcheck_polys_batch_eval\n110\t\n111\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n112\t\n113\t#### 4a: Batch Case A (late_eval) main_ptrs\n114\t\n115\tAfter the Case A loop (current lines 1173-1211), instead of calling `main_ptrs.to_device()` per trace:\n116\t\n117\t1. Collect all Case A main_ptrs into a flat `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets.\n118\t2. After the loop, do a single `all_late_main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n119\t3. Each `late_eval` TraceCtx gets `main_ptrs_ptr` pointing into the shared buffer at its offset.\n120\t4. Store the shared buffer as `_keepalive_late_main_ptrs` to keep the device memory alive.\n121\t\n122\t```rust\n123\t// Before Case A loop (pre-size based on trace count estimate):\n124\tlet mut all_late_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::with_capacity(self.n_per_trace.len() * 2);\n125\tlet mut late_main_ptrs_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\n126\t\n127\t// Inside Case A (round == n_lift + 1):\n128\tlate_main_ptrs_offsets.push(all_late_main_ptrs.len());\n129\tlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..].iter()\n130\t    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: m.buffer().as_ptr(), air_width: ... })\n131\t    .collect_vec();\n132\tall_late_main_ptrs.extend(main_ptrs);\n133\t// Don't call to_device() yet — defer to after the loop\n134\t\n135\t// After Case A loop:\n136\tlet _keepalive_late_main_ptrs = if !all_late_main_ptrs.is_empty() {\n137\t    let buf = all_late_main_ptrs.to_device()?;\n138\t    // Backfill pointers into late_eval TraceCtx entries\n139\t    for (i, t) in late_eval.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n140\t        t.main_ptrs_ptr = unsafe { buf.as_ptr().add(late_main_ptrs_offsets[i]) };\n141\t    }\n142\t    Some(buf)\n143\t} else {\n144\t    None\n145\t};\n146\t```\n147\t\n148\t**Implementation note**: The Case A loop currently pushes to `late_eval` inside the loop. We need to restructure slightly: defer `late_eval.push()` until after the device upload, or push with a placeholder pointer and backfill. The backfill approach is simpler — push with `std::ptr::null()` initially, then fill in after upload.\n149\t\n150\t#### 4b: Batch Case B (early_eval) main_ptrs\n151\t\n152\tAfter Phase 3 (current lines 1303-1364), instead of calling `main_ptrs.to_device()` per trace:\n153\t\n154\t1. Extend the Phase 3 loop to collect all Case B main_ptrs into a flat `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets.\n155\t2. After the loop, do a single `all_early_main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n156\t3. Each `early_eval` TraceCtx gets `main_ptrs_ptr` pointing into the shared buffer at its offset.\n157\t4. Store the shared buffer as `_keepalive_early_main_ptrs`.\n158\t\n159\t```rust\n160\t// Before Phase 3 loop (pre-size based on Case B trace count):\n161\tlet mut all_early_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::with_capacity(case_b_traces.len() * 2);\n162\tlet mut early_main_ptrs_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::with_capacity(case_b_traces.len());\n163\t\n164\t// Inside Phase 3 per-trace loop:\n165\tearly_main_ptrs_offsets.push(all_early_main_ptrs.len());\n166\tlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..].iter()\n167\t    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: base_ptr.wrapping_add(...), air_width: ... })\n168\t    .collect_vec();\n169\tall_early_main_ptrs.extend(main_ptrs);\n170\t\n171\t// After Phase 3 loop:\n172\tlet _keepalive_early_main_ptrs = if !all_early_main_ptrs.is_empty() {\n173\t    let buf = all_early_main_ptrs.to_device()?;\n174\t    for (i, t) in early_eval.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n175\t        t.main_ptrs_ptr = unsafe { buf.as_ptr().add(early_main_ptrs_offsets[i]) };\n176\t    }\n177\t    Some(buf)\n178\t} else {\n179\t    None\n180\t};\n181\t```\n182\t\n183\t**Why**: This is the main optimization. ~310 Case B traces per round → 1 upload. The total data is ~310 × 2 entries × 16 bytes = ~10KB per round — a trivial single upload.\n184\t\n185\t### Change 5: Update test file\n186\t\n187\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/tests.rs:707-726`\n188\t\n189\tChange:\n190\t```rust\n191\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device().unwrap();\n192\t// ...\n193\tmain_ptrs_dev,\n194\t```\n195\tTo:\n196\t```rust\n197\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device().unwrap();\n198\t// ...\n199\tmain_ptrs_ptr: main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr(),\n200\t```\n201\t\n202\tAdd `_keepalive` binding to keep `main_ptrs_dev` alive for the test scope.\n203\t\n204\t**Why**: Test constructs TraceCtx directly and needs to match the new field name.\n205\t\n206\t## Invariants\n207\t\n208\t1. **Pointer validity**: Every `main_ptrs_ptr` in a TraceCtx must point into a device buffer that outlives all uses of that TraceCtx. The `_keepalive_*` variables in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` ensure this — they are declared before the evaluation calls and dropped at function exit.\n209\t\n210\t2. **Pointer alignment**: `MainMatrixPtrs<EF>` is `#[repr(C)]` with natural alignment. Pointers into a flat `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` are correctly aligned since the buffer is a contiguous array.\n211\t\n212\t3. **Correctness**: The raw pointer values are identical to what `.as_ptr()` returned from the individual `DeviceBuffer`s. The data content (matrix pointers + widths) is unchanged. GPU kernels receive the same pointer values.\n213\t\n214\t4. **No behavioral change for APC 0**: At APC 0, the multi-threading threshold is not met and there are few traces (~20 per segment). The batching still works but produces a small flat vector. No regression expected.\n215\t\n216\t5. **Fallback path correctness**: The fallback evaluation functions (`evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu`, `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu`) receive the same pointer value they received before. The only difference is the function signature changes from `&DeviceBuffer<T>` to `*const T`.\n217\t\n218\t## Measurement Plan\n219\t\n220\tRun the standard benchmark:\n221\t```bash\n222\tcd /home/georg/powdr && openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n223\t```\n224\t\n225\tAnalyze with spec.py:\n226\t```bash\n227\tpython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json apc300\n228\tpython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc100/metrics.json apc100\n229\tpython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json apc000\n230\t```\n231\t\n232\t### Success criteria\n233\t\n234\t| Metric | APC 300 Target | Rationale |\n235\t|--------|---------------|-----------|\n236\t| MLE Rounds | < 155ms | ≥10% improvement from 170ms (conservative, given ~3000-4000 eliminated calls) |\n237\t| STARK excl trace | < 1395ms | ≥12ms net improvement |\n238\t| STARK excl trace APC 0 | < 2205ms | No regression (< 3% increase from 2140ms) |\n239\t\n240\t### Verification\n241\t\n242\t1. Prove + verify passes for all 3 APC configs (0, 100, 300)\n243\t2. Run APC 300 three times to confirm consistency (MLE Rounds std < 10ms)\n244\t\n245\t## Rollback Criteria\n246\t\n247\tRevert if ANY of:\n248\t1. MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms (below measurement noise)\n249\t2. STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by > 65ms\n250\t3. Prove + verify fails for any APC config\n251\t4. GPU OOM at any APC config\n252\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6b231730-29d0-498c-8e87-666663f10549","uuid":"fdfa4cfb-d84f-4875-94a9-896020e7bf3c","timestamp":"2026-04-13T09:51:01.627Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-13-1430-batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Batch MLE main_ptrs Upload\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` overhead in MLE sumcheck rounds by consolidating all per-round main_ptrs into a single flat vector and uploading it with one `to_device()` call. This removes ~3000-4000 `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` + `cudaFreeAsync` triplets per benchmark, saving an estimated 20-40ms from the 170ms MLE Rounds span at APC 300.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry point\n\n`LogupZerocheckGpu::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1111`\n\nThis function is called once per MLE round per segment. At APC 300 with 2 segments and ~8 rounds per segment, it executes ~16 times per benchmark.\n\n### Per-trace main_ptrs upload (the bottleneck)\n\n**Case A (late_eval, round > n_lift)** — lines 1188-1195:\n```rust\nlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..]\n    .iter()\n    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: m.buffer().as_ptr(), air_width: ... })\n    .collect_vec();\nlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // ← per-trace cudaMallocAsync + H2D\n```\n\n**Case B (early_eval, round <= n_lift)** — lines 1335-1347:\n```rust\nlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..]\n    .iter()\n    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: base_ptr.wrapping_add(...), air_width: ... })\n    .collect_vec();\nlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // ← per-trace cudaMallocAsync + H2D\n```\n\nEach call creates a `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` via:\n1. `d_malloc()` acquires `MEMORY_MANAGER` mutex + `cudaMallocAsync` (~5-10μs)\n2. `copy_to()` → `cudaMemcpyAsync` H2D (~2-5μs)\n\nWhen each `DeviceBuffer` is dropped (at `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` exit), `d_free()` acquires the same mutex + calls `cudaFreeAsync`. So the full per-trace cost is: 2 mutex acquisitions + `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` + `cudaFreeAsync` + HashMap insert/remove, totaling ~15-20μs per trace.\n\n**Count**: Case A fires once per trace across all rounds (~310 per segment × 2 segments = ~620 calls). Case B fires for each trace where `round <= n_lift`, decreasing as rounds progress. Total across all rounds and segments: ~3000-4000 calls. At ~15-20μs per call including deallocation, this is ~45-80ms of overhead, of which ~30-40ms is on the critical path (allocation + copy; deallocation is deferred to function exit).\n\n### How main_ptrs_dev is consumed\n\n**TraceCtx struct** — `batch_mle.rs:89-105`:\n```rust\npub(crate) struct TraceCtx {\n    // ...\n    pub main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>,  // owns the allocation\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nAll consumers only use the raw pointer:\n1. **Batch builders** (`batch_mle.rs:188`, `batch_mle.rs:335`, `batch_mle_monomial.rs:184,407,672`): use `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()`\n2. **Single-trace fallback** (`batch_mle.rs:473`): calls `evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu(&t.main_ptrs_dev)`, which internally uses `.as_ptr()` at `mle_round.rs:56`\n3. **Single-trace fallback** (`batch_mle.rs:642`): calls `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu(&t.main_ptrs_dev)`, which internally uses `.as_ptr()` at `mle_round.rs:111`\n\nNeither fallback function uses `.len()` or any other `DeviceBuffer` method beyond `.as_ptr()`.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Modify TraceCtx to use raw pointer\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:89-105`\n\nReplace:\n```rust\npub main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>,\n```\nWith:\n```rust\npub main_ptrs_ptr: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>,\n```\n\n**Why**: TraceCtx no longer owns individual allocations. It holds a pointer into a shared device buffer that is kept alive by the caller.\n\n### Change 2: Update batch builder usage of main_ptrs\n\n**Files**:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:188` — `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new()`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:335` — `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new()`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:184` — `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::new()`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:407` — `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch::new()`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:672` — `LogupMonomialBatch::new()`\n\nChange `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr` at each site.\n\nAlso update the doc comment at `batch_mle_monomial.rs:109-110` which references `main_ptrs_dev` by name.\n\n**Why**: Trivial rename. The raw pointer is the same value that `.as_ptr()` returned.\n\n### Change 3: Update fallback evaluation function signatures\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mle_round.rs`\n\n`evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu()` (line 24): Change parameter from `d_main_ptrs: &DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to `d_main_ptrs: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>`. Update the call site at line 56 from `d_main_ptrs.as_ptr()` to `d_main_ptrs`.\n\n`evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu()` (line 78): Same change. Update call site at line 111.\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nUpdate call sites:\n- Line 473: `&t.main_ptrs_dev` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr`\n- Line 642: `&t.main_ptrs_dev` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr`\n\n**Why**: These functions only need the raw pointer. Removing the `DeviceBuffer` reference requirement allows them to work with shared-buffer pointers. This trades a nominal compile-time type-safety guarantee for the performance gain, but both functions are `pub(crate)` internal helpers already operating in an unsafe context (raw device pointers throughout).\n\n### Change 4: Batch main_ptrs upload in sumcheck_polys_batch_eval\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\n#### 4a: Batch Case A (late_eval) main_ptrs\n\nAfter the Case A loop (current lines 1173-1211), instead of calling `main_ptrs.to_device()` per trace:\n\n1. Collect all Case A main_ptrs into a flat `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets.\n2. After the loop, do a single `all_late_main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n3. Each `late_eval` TraceCtx gets `main_ptrs_ptr` pointing into the shared buffer at its offset.\n4. Store the shared buffer as `_keepalive_late_main_ptrs` to keep the device memory alive.\n\n```rust\n// Before Case A loop (pre-size based on trace count estimate):\nlet mut all_late_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::with_capacity(self.n_per_trace.len() * 2);\nlet mut late_main_ptrs_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\n\n// Inside Case A (round == n_lift + 1):\nlate_main_ptrs_offsets.push(all_late_main_ptrs.len());\nlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..].iter()\n    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: m.buffer().as_ptr(), air_width: ... })\n    .collect_vec();\nall_late_main_ptrs.extend(main_ptrs);\n// Don't call to_device() yet — defer to after the loop\n\n// After Case A loop:\nlet _keepalive_late_main_ptrs = if !all_late_main_ptrs.is_empty() {\n    let buf = all_late_main_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    // Backfill pointers into late_eval TraceCtx entries\n    for (i, t) in late_eval.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n        t.main_ptrs_ptr = unsafe { buf.as_ptr().add(late_main_ptrs_offsets[i]) };\n    }\n    Some(buf)\n} else {\n    None\n};\n```\n\n**Implementation note**: The Case A loop currently pushes to `late_eval` inside the loop. We need to restructure slightly: defer `late_eval.push()` until after the device upload, or push with a placeholder pointer and backfill. The backfill approach is simpler — push with `std::ptr::null()` initially, then fill in after upload.\n\n#### 4b: Batch Case B (early_eval) main_ptrs\n\nAfter Phase 3 (current lines 1303-1364), instead of calling `main_ptrs.to_device()` per trace:\n\n1. Extend the Phase 3 loop to collect all Case B main_ptrs into a flat `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets.\n2. After the loop, do a single `all_early_main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n3. Each `early_eval` TraceCtx gets `main_ptrs_ptr` pointing into the shared buffer at its offset.\n4. Store the shared buffer as `_keepalive_early_main_ptrs`.\n\n```rust\n// Before Phase 3 loop (pre-size based on Case B trace count):\nlet mut all_early_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::with_capacity(case_b_traces.len() * 2);\nlet mut early_main_ptrs_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::with_capacity(case_b_traces.len());\n\n// Inside Phase 3 per-trace loop:\nearly_main_ptrs_offsets.push(all_early_main_ptrs.len());\nlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..].iter()\n    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: base_ptr.wrapping_add(...), air_width: ... })\n    .collect_vec();\nall_early_main_ptrs.extend(main_ptrs);\n\n// After Phase 3 loop:\nlet _keepalive_early_main_ptrs = if !all_early_main_ptrs.is_empty() {\n    let buf = all_early_main_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    for (i, t) in early_eval.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n        t.main_ptrs_ptr = unsafe { buf.as_ptr().add(early_main_ptrs_offsets[i]) };\n    }\n    Some(buf)\n} else {\n    None\n};\n```\n\n**Why**: This is the main optimization. ~310 Case B traces per round → 1 upload. The total data is ~310 × 2 entries × 16 bytes = ~10KB per round — a trivial single upload.\n\n### Change 5: Update test file\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/tests.rs:707-726`\n\nChange:\n```rust\nlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device().unwrap();\n// ...\nmain_ptrs_dev,\n```\nTo:\n```rust\nlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device().unwrap();\n// ...\nmain_ptrs_ptr: main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr(),\n```\n\nAdd `_keepalive` binding to keep `main_ptrs_dev` alive for the test scope.\n\n**Why**: Test constructs TraceCtx directly and needs to match the new field name.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Pointer validity**: Every `main_ptrs_ptr` in a TraceCtx must point into a device buffer that outlives all uses of that TraceCtx. The `_keepalive_*` variables in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` ensure this — they are declared before the evaluation calls and dropped at function exit.\n\n2. **Pointer alignment**: `MainMatrixPtrs<EF>` is `#[repr(C)]` with natural alignment. Pointers into a flat `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` are correctly aligned since the buffer is a contiguous array.\n\n3. **Correctness**: The raw pointer values are identical to what `.as_ptr()` returned from the individual `DeviceBuffer`s. The data content (matrix pointers + widths) is unchanged. GPU kernels receive the same pointer values.\n\n4. **No behavioral change for APC 0**: At APC 0, the multi-threading threshold is not met and there are few traces (~20 per segment). The batching still works but produces a small flat vector. No regression expected.\n\n5. **Fallback path correctness**: The fallback evaluation functions (`evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu`, `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu`) receive the same pointer value they received before. The only difference is the function signature changes from `&DeviceBuffer<T>` to `*const T`.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun the standard benchmark:\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr && openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n```\n\nAnalyze with spec.py:\n```bash\npython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json apc300\npython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc100/metrics.json apc100\npython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json apc000\n```\n\n### Success criteria\n\n| Metric | APC 300 Target | Rationale |\n|--------|---------------|-----------|\n| MLE Rounds | < 155ms | ≥10% improvement from 170ms (conservative, given ~3000-4000 eliminated calls) |\n| STARK excl trace | < 1395ms | ≥12ms net improvement |\n| STARK excl trace APC 0 | < 2205ms | No regression (< 3% increase from 2140ms) |\n\n### Verification\n\n1. Prove + verify passes for all 3 APC configs (0, 100, 300)\n2. Run APC 300 three times to confirm consistency (MLE Rounds std < 10ms)\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of:\n1. MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms (below measurement noise)\n2. STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by > 65ms\n3. Prove + verify fails for any APC config\n4. GPU OOM at any APC config\n","numLines":252,"startLine":1,"totalLines":252}}}
