{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FsP7xsDLM3EY5MSgLCgTrD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me compare the results:\n\n**APC 300:**\n- Before: MLE Rounds = 170ms, STARK excl trace = 1417ms\n- After: MLE Rounds = 165ms, STARK excl trace = 1421ms\n- Change: MLE Rounds -5ms, STARK excl trace +4ms (within noise)\n\n**APC 100:**\n- Before: MLE Rounds = 140ms, STARK excl trace = 1583ms\n- After: MLE Rounds = 135ms, STARK excl trace = 1593ms\n\n\n- Change: MLE Rounds -5ms, STARK excl trace +10ms (within noise)\n\n**APC 0:**\n- Before: MLE Rounds = 114ms, STARK excl trace = 2147ms\n- After: MLE Rounds = 113ms, STARK excl trace = 2150ms\n- Change: MLE Rounds -1ms, STARK excl trace +3ms (within noise)\n\nThe improvements are marginal across all configurations—MLE Rounds only dropped 5ms at best, which falls short of the rollback threshold requiring at least 10ms improvement. I should check whether this meets the rollback criteria.\n\nLooking at the rollback conditions, the first criterion explicitly states that MLE Rounds improvements below 10ms at APC 300 trigger a revert, and that's exactly what happened here. The H2D copy count did decrease substantially from 30035 to 24436, confirming the batching mechanism is functioning, but the actual performance gain suggests the per-call overhead was much lower than anticipated—closer to 1 microsecond rather than the 15-20 microseconds we estimated.\n\nLet me run this one more time to verify the results.\n\nActually, wait—the plan already specifies running APC 300 three times to check consistency, but since the improvement is clearly below the 10ms threshold, the variance is irrelevant. Even in the best-case scenario, we wouldn't meet the threshold.\n\nSo the path forward is clear: I'll document this in the report, commit the implementation to preserve it in git history, then revert the changes on the branch and create the tag.\n\nBut looking at the actual numbers more carefully, I'm noticing something interesting—the improvement is consistent at exactly -5ms across both APC 300 and APC 100, which suggests this might be a real signal rather than noise, even if it's smaller than expected.\n\nHowever, the plan is clear: if the improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms, I should revert. At 5ms, that threshold is crossed. The nsys profiling confirmed the optimization is mechanically sound—we did reduce H2D copies by ~5600—but the per-call CUDA overhead turned out to be much lower than anticipated, around 1μs instead of 15-20μs. The improvement is genuine, just too modest to justify the added complexity, so I'll move forward with the revert.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2714,"cache_read_input_tokens":91003,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2714},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6b231730-29d0-498c-8e87-666663f10549","uuid":"ae1a11b0-1dc4-41f4-ab0d-80f0eebbf8da"}
